data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In **EF Core 9** (.NET 9), the Cosmos DB provider started throwing `InvalidOperationException` on synchronous LINQ operations by default. You could opt back in via:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(..., cosmosOptions =>
    cosmosOptions.AllowSynchronousQueryExecution(true));
```

In **.NET 11 / EF Core 11**, that **sync-over-async escape hatch is removed entirely**. All four of your methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime with no workaround.

## The fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()` + `await`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()` + `await`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()` + `await`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()` + `await`
- Return types become `Task<T>` / `Task`
- Method names should follow the `*Async` convention

The async methods come from `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` (ensure `using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;` is present). Note that callers must also go async — this tends to propagate up the call stack ("async all the way").
